00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.660
So without further delay,
I think the key concept

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.820
that folks need to keep
in mind, as they're

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.300
moving into the Public Cloud,
is security will be top of mind.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.610
And your security
in the Public Cloud

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.410
can be same as what
you have on-prem.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.470
It could be better than
what you have on-prem.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.510
But the way security
will be implemented

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.670
in Public Clouds would
be different than what

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.899
you have done on-prem.

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:24.190
As an example, in Google
Cloud, there a lot of services

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:25.360
you could use.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.550
You could be using GCE, GKE,
or you could be using Storage.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:35.230
And for example, for GCES
and other storage services,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.830
the access to those services
is controlled through APIs.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.640
So when you're thinking about
securing those services,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.220
you have to think about what
services, what controls,

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.560
what policies you
would have in mind,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.710
so people don't have access
to the storage that is being

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.374
managed by Google for you.

00:01:53.374 --> 00:01:54.790
There are a couple
of key concepts

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.165
that you have to
think about when

00:01:56.165 --> 00:01:59.600
you're thinking about security
for your applications.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.160
One is, how do you protect
from the edge of the network

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.930
to close to your workload?

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.081
That's the core of your network,
or to the endpoint itself.

00:02:08.081 --> 00:02:09.580
So there will be
layers of security,

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.790
and I'll double-click to
explain what the layers are.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.760
And the other key
concept to think about

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.270
is there will be different
levels of security rules

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:19.940
you would apply.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.680
So it will start from course
rules that apply to everything,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.050
and then they'll move on
to more fine-grained rules

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.260
that you can apply to control
at the microservice level.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.972
So as you're thinking about
how to take care of security,

00:02:32.972 --> 00:02:35.305
you ought to broadly think
about some of these concepts.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.290
So just double-clicking
to give you guys

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.150
a quick flavor of
what this would mean.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.210
So for example, if you're
protecting your web

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.410
application, you
have to think about,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.340
how do I do DDoS
mitigation, or how do I

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.340
protect my applications
from layer 7 attacks?

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.050
And you would do
that by, for example,

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.720
using the global load
balancer we have,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.730
and the Cloud Armor
product that was

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.430
part of the keynote today,
and the IAP product, as well.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.680
So you would use that product.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.990
You could have rules--
layer 3, layer 4 rules,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.670
or layer 7 rules--

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.460
that will protect the
public-facing, internet-facing

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.720
applications you have.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.050
So that will be the protection
at the edge that I spoke about.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.610
And then, you will bring it
closer to your VPC parameter

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.520
and think about, at your VPC
perimeter level, what kind

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.320
of securities do you build?

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.520
And how do I lock in
access to my data?

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.910
How do I lock in my access
to the applications?

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.670
And you will do that
with VPC-level firewalls.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.800
This is where you
could have rules

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.370
like, I only want HTTPS traffic
coming in from the internet,

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:43.260
so it will implement those
rules at your VPC level.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.990
And the example that
I shared about GCS,

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.460
and having controls
on who to access this,

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.580
you will do that using
the VPC Service Controls.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.384
So those are the
key concepts you

00:03:53.384 --> 00:03:55.800
have to think about when you're
protecting your perimeter.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.310
Use the VPC Service Controls,
use the native firewall

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.310
that we have.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.100
Then, taking it to
the next level down,

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.920
now most customers would
want to have more detail,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.910
more fine-grained controls.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.620
Controls like add to your
service account level,

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.720
what your service accounts
can do, cannot do.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.560
What services they can touch,
what data they can access.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.880
So you would have the next
level of security there at GKE

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.340
level, at different
IAM policy level,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.200
to bring your controls
to that level.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.220
And all of this is
greatly complemented

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.044
by partner products, so there
are native Google products that

00:04:34.044 --> 00:04:35.960
will provide you different
levels of security,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.690
but you should complement them
with partner products that

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:39.620
are--

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.290
we have a broad ecosystem
of partner products,

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:45.800
and you should think about
how partner products would

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.770
help you complete your
security story for GCP.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.670
So I'll just show you
a quick picture on--

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.730
I will not cover all
these boxes here,

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.650
but this is just a
quick review for you

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:01.980
to explain how partner products
complement what Google does.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.580
So there is a foundation
infrastructure level

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:05.970
that is covered by Google.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.800
But if you go above that
level into Storage Network,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.090
you will add on partner
products to provide

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.360
complementary security services.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.260
You know, things
like brand products

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:18.430
like next-generation
firewalls is something--

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.380
you would work with companies
like Palo Alto Networks,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.260
and use their production
and their products

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.919
to complement the products
and services that Google

00:05:26.919 --> 00:05:27.710
has to protect you.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:30.080
So your overall
security architecture

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.960
would be a combination of
what we natively provide

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.090
and partner products
that we have.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.620
So I think those were the key
concepts I wanted to cover,

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.270
I think especially for
folks who are new to GCP.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.590
So they understand how
we think about security,

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:50.600
how partners come into
the picture, and so on.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.940
One key concept that
I wanted to add on

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.750
before we call in the panelists
was the shared VPC concept.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.130
It's a very powerful concept.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:00.920
If you haven't
looked at it so far,

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.530
I would recommend
you take a look.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:08.390
And just taking a step back
of why this is powerful,

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:10.700
the key problem that
people had in public clouds

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:14.180
was in every department, every
app owner taking your credit

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:15.710
card, creating
their own projects,

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.750
and there would be
thousands of projects.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.850
And the central
security team, IT team,

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:22.040
would have no control
on those projects.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.510
In Google, that
problem was solved

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:27.020
using the organization concept,
and having a shared VPC

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.910
architecture, where
you would have a host

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:32.630
VPC that your security
team, your IT teams,

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:35.600
can implement their
policies, define the subnets,

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.710
have a next-gen firewall like
Palo Alto in your host VPC,

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.470
and have a centralized
way of managing policies

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.410
for all the service
projects that your app

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:46.370
owners are creating.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:49.100
So your app owners can
have the flexibility

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.900
of creating the
projects they want,

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.750
without having to
worry about security.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:57.740
And you, as the security
admin, have the control,

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:02.810
using this shared VPC concept,
by having your security defined

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.400
at the host project level.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:07.400
And the routing of all the
traffic to the host project,

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:11.770
having centralized policies
are done in one place.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:15.432
And when I explain this concept
to our partners, key customers,

00:07:15.432 --> 00:07:17.140
they are saying, hey,
this is the closest

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:20.440
we have seen we can get to
the old on-prem, centralized

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:21.220
control model.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.140
So this is a very
powerful concept.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.420
It helps you do
security at scale,

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.320
and from a centralized fashion.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.490
So if you haven't taken
a look at the shared VPC,

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:31.330
please take a look.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:34.750
It also gels very well
with our partner products,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:36.610
like the Palo Alto
VM series firewall

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:40.730
and they plug in beautifully
into the shared VPC model.

