you don't want to be experts
in everything, right?

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.358
So the things that
you can actually

00:12:03.358 --> 00:12:05.250
farm off to another
company, the better off

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:06.300
you're actually going to be.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.425
So this way, you can actually
focus on the business

00:12:08.425 --> 00:12:11.080
and actually focus
on technology.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.230
So that was one of
the big drivers.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:17.659
And in Palo Alto, we already
had Palo Alto in-house.

00:12:17.659 --> 00:12:19.950
And as soon as we found out--
because we basically went

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.392
to the Google Cloud in January.

00:12:22.392 --> 00:12:23.850
And then we had to
have it stood up

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:27.390
by Mother's Day, which was
basically by the end of April.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.490
So within four
months, we basically

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:30.930
decided to go with Google Cloud.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:32.955
And then we actually
started to roll it out.

00:12:32.955 --> 00:12:35.580
And as soon as we did that, they
started talking with Palo Alto

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.040
and see if they had
actually a beta program.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.372
And then we actually got it
[? running ?] from there.

00:12:40.372 --> 00:12:42.080
VINEET BHAN: And how
was the Mother's Day

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.160
experience for you?

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.399
Were things smooth?

00:12:44.399 --> 00:12:45.690
BILL O'BRIEN: It was, actually.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:46.190
Yeah.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:48.560
So we actually had
Akamai in front of it.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:50.960
So we were actually able to
load how much we were going

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.335
to send to the legacy site,
and how much we were actually

00:12:53.335 --> 00:12:55.170
going to send to GCP.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.539
So at what point--

00:12:57.539 --> 00:12:59.330
if you actually put
the domain in directly,

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.417
we actually had 100% of
that traffic going into GCP.

00:13:02.417 --> 00:13:03.750
VINEET BHAN: Great to hear that.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.810
Thank you.

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:08.080
And so Bill, one more
question for you,

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.940
just double-clicking on
what you have on GCP.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.180
What kind of
workloads do you run?

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:18.110
And how much are you guys
into containers, GKE?

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.690
Any insights that you can
share with the audience?

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:21.481
BILL O'BRIEN: Yeah.

00:13:21.481 --> 00:13:23.030
We're extensively GKE.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.640
So when we actually
first spun it up,

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:27.950
we wanted to use shared VPCs.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:31.450
But at that time, shared VPCs
weren't available onto the GKE

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:32.480
clusters.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:33.980
So what we ended
up having to do was

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:38.090
we ended up having to create
VPNs within GCP itself

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:42.744
to go from the shared VPC into
the GCP into the GKE cluster.

00:13:42.744 --> 00:13:44.660
But now, they've actually
brought that inline.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.576
And one of the biggest
things that we actually

00:13:46.576 --> 00:13:48.650
saw with Google was
how quickly they're

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:51.350
able to move things
from alpha to beta

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:54.470
to actually having
it provisioned out.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:57.671
And even-- each day you go
onto the Google console,

00:13:57.671 --> 00:13:59.420
and all of a sudden,
you see a new pop-up.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:00.650
And it's just like,
hey, what is this?

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:01.955
What is this going to be doing?

00:14:01.955 --> 00:14:03.920
And all of a sudden,
then that's where

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.600
IAM comes really into play.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:10.010
Because the more people have
the ability to make changes,

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.510
the more dangerous
it's going to be.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.750
So all of a sudden, as new
features get turned up,

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:16.130
you want to be able to
make sure that you actually

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:17.000
control the access.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:18.840
Because all of a sudden,
they're going to be like, hey,

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:19.610
what does this button do?

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:21.401
And all of a sudden,
they'll click on that.

00:14:21.401 --> 00:14:23.030
And then you won't
know what happened.

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:23.905
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:14:23.905 --> 00:14:24.630
Thank you.

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:25.819
So, Bill, question for you.

00:14:25.819 --> 00:14:27.860
Similarly, if you could
double-click on what kind

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:31.460
of deployment you have and what
you're using for GKE or any

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:32.750
other--

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:34.490
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT: Rob, sorry.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:35.740
Yeah.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.710
Right now, we have a lot
of non-production workloads

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:39.770
out there.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.250
My two experts in this
space, actually three,

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:44.360
are in the front two rows here.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.510
So if we get to the Q&amp;A,
I might bring them up

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.750
to answer more
detailed questions.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:53.320
But really, I would start
with non-production,

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:55.790
obviously, get familiar with it.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:15:00.810
And if you have the DevOps
mentality or methodology,

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:04.120
make sure that you have
very good ops, not just dev.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:08.970
So understand routing,
networking, at the start of it.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:12.370
That will definitely
help you out.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:13.390
VINEET BHAN: Thank you.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.515
Sanjay, a question for you.

00:15:14.515 --> 00:15:16.430
You must be talking
to a lot of customers

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:19.100
and hearing from them about
how they're moving into Cloud.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.930
Any insights, customer
stories you have on where

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:23.750
you see customers heading?

00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:27.530
And what are the key trends
there, or key challenges,

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:28.770
that you're seeing?

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:29.630
SANJAY GUPTA: Sure.

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:30.740
Thanks, Neet.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:36.320
So clearly, I think everyone
knows that Cloud is definitely

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:39.990
getting more mainstream, if not
in terms of real deployments,

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:43.330
but I definitely see
in terms of interest.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:48.290
Containers are, I'd say,
relatively new, or newer.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:52.410
So not many customers are, I'd
say, onto it at this point.

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:56.420
But two main
perspectives I would

00:15:56.420 --> 00:16:04.150
like to share, which strike
me when I speak to customers.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:06.150
The first one is
about the difference

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.400
in perspective,
or point of view,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.270
between the business
and the CSOs.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.510
So businesses typically think
that the IT and the security

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.920
is not moving fast enough.

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:21.890
While, if you talk to a lot of
CSOs and the security folks,

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:26.950
they think that the business
is pulling them in very hard.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:31.300
So there's this big disconnect
between the speed at which

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:34.150
the business wants to move and
the speed at which the security

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:36.280
folks want to move, right?

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:41.800
And I'd say that the relatively
few successful organizations

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:43.930
I've seen is where the
security folks actually

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:45.700
have taken the baton.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:49.327
And they are essentially moving
at the same speed, or even

00:16:49.327 --> 00:16:50.410
faster, than the business.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:52.270
And those organizations,
I can see,

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:57.970
are really being very proactive,
and moving and innovating

00:16:57.970 --> 00:16:59.840
at a faster speed.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:01.870
The second perspective
which I see

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:04.374
is the fact that it's like--

00:17:04.374 --> 00:17:05.770
is the chasm.

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:08.890
It's a chasm between the folks
who have jumped onto the Cloud,

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:10.900
and they have started
their cloud journey,

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:14.010
versus the folks who have not.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:17.319
And one of my realizations
has been that, hey, Cloud

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:18.550
is obviously all about speed.

00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:20.740
But it's also about
innovation and being

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.839
able to adapt and iterate.

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:26.109
Because I'd say that most
Cloud providers, including

00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:28.089
Google itself, as
Bill just mentioned,

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:31.720
they're coming out with new
services at a phenomenal rate.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:36.810
So you should be willing
to both apply security

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:40.870
at the right speed, as
well as willing to iterate

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:43.780
with these Cloud providers and
improve your security controls

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:46.695
and security
mechanisms over time.

00:17:46.695 --> 00:17:47.570
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:48.500
Thank you, Sanjay.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:50.480
So, Sanjay, one more
question for you.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.580
So as customers and
folks in the audience

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:55.010
are thinking about
security, what

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:56.750
are your thoughts
about what key things

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:58.430
they need to keep
in mind when they're

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.930
thinking about security-- when
they're thinking about moving

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:02.939
more and more workloads
into the Cloud?

00:18:02.939 --> 00:18:05.480
What are the key things that
you would recommend them to keep

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:08.450
at the top of their list?

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:09.280
SANJAY GUPTA: Sure.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:14.220
So I'll start off and extend
what I was saying before.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:15.760
So the first thing is--

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.050
so I'd say there are
two things overall.

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:19.940
One is speed.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:23.510
And second is new and
different attack surfaces.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:26.780
So as you're moving into
Cloud, and your applications

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:29.090
and workloads into
Cloud, you have

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:31.400
to realize that Cloud
is all about speed.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:34.070
So that means that
the security also

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:38.060
has to be able to move at
the speed of the Cloud.

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:42.980
Which means that the security
has to get highly automated,

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:45.780
like your applications
and workloads are.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.470
Number two, the security has
to become more Cloud native,

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:53.210
which basically means it has to
get integrated into your Cloud

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:55.629
environments and leveraging
the Cloud services,

00:18:55.629 --> 00:18:57.420
which the Cloud
infrastructure is offering.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:00.710
And that is going to give
it that Cloud native flavor.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:03.740
And the last, and this one is
that security fundamentally

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.960
has to shift left,
which basically

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:10.260
means security has to get
more ingrained with the AppDev

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:11.600
lifecycle.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:16.100
It shouldn't remain disjointed
from the application,

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:18.830
development, and
deployment itself.

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:21.470
And that is why, at
Palo Alto Networks,

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:25.080
we lay a lot of
emphasis on that.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.680
And there are a lot of
product capabilities,

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:32.030
including automation, tight
integration with Google Cloud,

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:36.650
as well as making our
policies more and more

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:42.320
automated is what we invest
on on the product side.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:48.980
And then, the second aspect
is that, as you are thinking

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:50.960
about the Cloud
security, you have

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:53.607
to realize that there are
some things which have changed

00:19:53.607 --> 00:19:54.440
in the cloud, right?

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.050
Which means that there are
some attack surfaces which have

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:59.570
gotten minimized or gone away.

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:03.810
As Vineet was showing the slide
earlier, the infrastructure,

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:07.130
which is the ownership of the
Cloud providers-- of Google,

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:08.360
in this case--

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:13.100
is going to be secured
by Google themselves.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:16.400
But then, there are new attack
surfaces which have opened up,

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:19.874
which basically means
things like past services

00:20:19.874 --> 00:20:22.040
that you're going to consume
in Google Cloud, right?

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:26.720
And you need to think about
how to secure those services.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.850
And that may mean
that you will have

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:31.790
to look at new mechanisms
and ways, particularly more

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:36.030
API-centric mechanisms and ways,
to secure your environment.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.500
So those are the two
main points, in summary.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.146
One is the speed
and how you make

00:20:40.146 --> 00:20:42.020
sure that your security
operates at the speed

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:43.310
of the application.

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:49.400
And number two, be very aware
of the way the attack surfaces

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:51.260
have changed in
this environment.

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:54.402
And then how you can secure
those new attack surfaces.

00:20:54.402 --> 00:20:55.610
VINEET BHAN: Perfect, Sanjay.

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.900
Those were great insights.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.450
So, Bill, a question for you.

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:02.070
You decided to go
down to GKE path.

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:06.080
What were the key security
aspects or concerns

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:08.660
you had to think about when
you decided to go production?

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:11.570
And you guys had a
limited time frame

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:12.980
to get the security
right, as you

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:15.170
were ramping up production
and for Mother's Day.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:17.949
So can you share some
key insights there?

00:21:17.949 --> 00:21:18.740
BILL O'BRIEN: Yeah.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:22.520
So with GKE, they
primarily went with GKE

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:26.490
because of the security that was
already handled within Google.

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:29.229
So because they've already had
that pretty much established,

00:21:29.229 --> 00:21:31.020
it was good for them
to be able to use that

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:33.560
and for us to be able to go
and move forward with that.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:37.760
So because we had such
a short time window,

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:40.520
we basically-- what we did was
we stood up a QA environment

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:41.610
for them.

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:45.110
So we spun up a QA environment
that had a VPN connection back

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:47.420
onto on-prem, so
they could actually

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:49.670
start pushing out their code,
building their clusters,

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:51.210
and start doing a
lot of development

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.909
and so forth, till we actually
figured out what the production

00:21:53.909 --> 00:21:55.700
environment was actually
going to look like

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:58.470
and how big it
actually needed to be.

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:00.440
And from the slide that
was up there before,

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:04.700
with the VM series that we ended
up spinning up to Palo Alto

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.650
and having multiple
interfaces off of it.

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:09.680
So we had production, we
had pre-prod, we had PCI.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:12.320
And then we basically
had a services one.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:15.700
And then there was
a DMZ side, as well.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:17.660
And on the DMZ side,
that was basically

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:20.900
where we had the shared VPC
to allow you to connect back

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:21.710
onto on-prem.

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:26.030
So within the GKE,
it had to do a lot

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.130
with the controls
and the clusters

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:29.370
and everything like that.

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:35.540
So it just provided a lot of
out-of-the-box features that

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:39.830
just allowed the management for
us to be spun up very quickly.

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:41.960
And then it took a lot of
things off of our plates.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:44.376
So that way, then, we don't
have to worry about OS patches

00:22:44.376 --> 00:22:45.600
and things like that.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:48.170
And then also, it handles
the internal load balancing

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:49.515
and things like that, as well.

00:22:49.515 --> 00:22:50.390
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:51.950
Thank you.

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:53.810
So, Rob, similar
question for you.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:56.210
As you guys were
implementing, how

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:58.130
did you think
about what products

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:02.129
you would use within
Google or partner products?

00:23:02.129 --> 00:23:03.170
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT: Yeah.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.850
I think, first, the key
is having that partnership

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:08.250
with the CSO organization.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:11.550
Our vice president spoke
yesterday, John Latchaw,

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.370
and talked about,
take your CSO out

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:18.690
for a few beers, or his
favorite beverage, repeatedly.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.160
Keep them on your side.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.620
That partnership is
critical from the start.

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:26.550
You don't want to come in
and have to bolt on security,

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.530
or be given a stack
of requirements later.

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:32.160
So really, partner
with the security team.

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:33.990
So our journey was
really good because we

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.820
have this great
partnership at Cardinal

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:38.770
Health between the teams.

00:23:38.770 --> 00:23:41.780
And we found that regularly
meeting during weekly stand

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:45.790
ups, just working together,
really made a huge difference.

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:49.080
So as we started looking
at Cloud Native first--

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:51.390
that's always what
were challenged to do--

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:56.760
what can you secure, by default,
within the cloud provider?

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.220
And then, if you get through
all those requirements,

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:02.940
you do identify a few gaps,
where something like Palo Alto

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:06.810
is required, primarily
ingress and egress protection

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:08.500
from the internet.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:13.110
So in a highly regulated
environment, HIPPA, GDPR now,

00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:16.175
you have to have those
protections in place.

00:24:16.175 --> 00:24:17.800
But I would urge
everybody in the room,

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:21.350
look at the Cloud Native first.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:22.890
Stretch that to its
limits, and then

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:24.348
start to look at
the third parties.

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:28.502
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:24:28.502 --> 00:24:31.050
So thanks for that.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:33.690
So going back to
Sanjay-- to you.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.090
From Palo Alto Network's
perspective, what are your--

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:39.620
I think you shared
a lot of insights.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:41.520
Are there any
recommendations, in terms of,

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:43.920
as the customer is going
through this cloud journey,

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:47.719
on how they should interface
with you and Google?

00:24:47.719 --> 00:24:49.260
Any other recommendations
they should

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:53.119
keep in mind on how to go
about thinking about security?

00:24:53.119 --> 00:24:53.910
SANJAY GUPTA: Sure.

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:55.080
Thanks, Neet.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:25:00.330
So fundamentally, I think
that, in many respects,

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.090
going to Cloud from a
security perspective

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:05.760
is no different than
previously, which

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:09.650
is that the core
principles of security

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:13.470
still apply, and are as
relevant to Cloud as they

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:15.510
were for any environment.

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:18.450
And those core principles being,
which we all really believed

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:22.770
in, is the fact that A, you need
to have complete visibility,

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:25.840
in terms of what is going
on in that environment.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:29.700
B, you need to be able to,
from that point onwards,

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:32.550
be able to minimize
your attack surface,

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:35.760
which is basically the security
principle of least privilege.

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:39.450
So that means that only
the necessary activity,

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:40.950
whether it's the
traffic, it's user

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:44.110
activity, it's transactions, is
happening in that environment.

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:47.130
And then, once you
have that as the base,

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:49.969
then you manage any known
threats in that environment.

00:25:49.969 --> 00:25:52.260
So you can block them and
control them and prevent them

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:53.116
right away.

00:25:53.116 --> 00:25:54.990
And then, on top of
that, we have the ability

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:58.660
to be able to detect and
remediate any unknown threats.

00:25:58.660 --> 00:26:00.660
So those are the core
security principles

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:03.030
that we have been operating
on for the last 12 years,

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:05.610
since Palo Alto Networks
have been founded.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.620
And I believe that they're
equally applicable to Cloud.

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:11.670
The thing which is
different about the Cloud

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:15.730
is, how do you
apply those security

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:18.440
principles and those security
controls in this environment?

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:21.400
Which is, essentially,
about making sure

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:24.700
that these things can be applied
at the speed of the cloud.

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:28.120
And these things can be applied
across all attack surfaces,

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:29.830
even some of those
new attack surfaces

00:26:29.830 --> 00:26:32.770
I was talking about previously.

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:37.270
So specifically,
our recommendation

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:39.940
for cloud security
is that, hey, to be

00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:42.040
able to achieve that,
you need to look

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:47.560
at multiple dimensions
of your cloud deployment,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:51.990
which is network, end
point, and the APIs.

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:54.075
So on the network--

00:26:54.075 --> 00:26:55.890
as Rob was mentioning,
on the network,

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.750
you should be able to
get visibility and be

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:01.110
able to control your
inbound and outbound traffic

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:03.600
into your cloud deployment,
as well as be able

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:07.230
to segment, across multiple
applications or workloads that

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:09.989
may be deployed in the cloud
environment, what Vineet was

00:27:09.989 --> 00:27:11.280
talking about-- the shared VPC.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:13.470
That's a great step
forward from Google,

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.450
which allows you
to be able to do

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:18.960
that in a much more effective
and efficient fashion.

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:22.400
So with that you can
have a good control

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:25.560
on who can access
your applications

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.720
and what kind of
access is happening

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.120
for those applications.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:32.280
Beyond that, on
the endpoint, you

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:36.720
need to make sure that there is
no malicious activity happening

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:39.250
and there is no [INAUDIBLE]
exploits happening.

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:41.100
So you need to have
mechanisms so as

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:44.850
to be able to control the
security and the endpoint.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:47.760
And then, lastly,
it's about the fact

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:51.960
that, as you go more
and more Cloud Native,

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:53.760
you will actually
consume more and more

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:57.894
of Cloud Native services,
which is the great services

00:27:57.894 --> 00:28:00.060
that the Cloud providers--
that Google-- is building

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:00.990
into their environments.

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:02.156
You would use their storage.

00:28:02.156 --> 00:28:05.070
You would use their database
as a service, right?

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:08.070
You will use managed
GKE as a service.

00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:13.250
And as you do that,
you may, in some sense,

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:17.520
lose complete visibility, in
terms of what is going on.

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:21.660
And you need a mechanism
through which you can basically

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:24.120
understand what is going
on in this environment,

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:27.814
and be able to apply
your corporate policies

00:28:27.814 --> 00:28:29.730
on the data which is
being stored and operated

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.210
upon in these environments.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.900
And the best way to
do that is a platform,

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:39.240
like Google provides
very rich APIs,

00:28:39.240 --> 00:28:41.070
and that is what
we have looked at.

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:44.460
And via APIs, through
tools like Evident,

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:47.022
we can actually give you
that complete visibility

00:28:47.022 --> 00:28:48.480
and monitoring of
what is happening

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:51.150
in these environments,
as well as

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:55.080
let you apply the
enterprise policies,

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.400
in terms of what is allowed
and what is not allowed.

00:28:57.400 --> 00:28:59.790
And if anything happens
outside of the norm,

00:28:59.790 --> 00:29:01.710
you can auto-remediate.

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:05.490
So for example, you
could say, you know what?

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:08.640
I do not want any workload which
is getting new workload, which

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:11.400
is getting deployed
in my Google VPC,

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:14.130
to have internet access
unless it has been

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:16.410
approved by the policy, per se.

00:29:16.410 --> 00:29:18.450
And if anything
like this happens,

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:21.240
then automatically stop and
prevent that from happening,

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:23.850
or automatically deploy a
next-generation firewall

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:25.680
in front of the
workload, so that you

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.310
have complete control over the
ingress and egress traffic.

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:33.760
So that is what is important.

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:38.040
So in summary, I believe
that, in a cloud deployment,

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:40.230
the same security
principles apply.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:43.380
And you need to look at it from
all the dimensions of network,

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:44.575
endpoint, and APIs.

00:29:44.575 --> 00:29:45.450
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:46.395
Thank you.

00:29:46.395 --> 00:29:50.787
And I completely agree
with what Sanjay said.

00:29:50.787 --> 00:29:52.620
We've been working
closely with our partners

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:55.770
to make sure the partner
products are integrated.

00:29:55.770 --> 00:29:58.350
And we think about security
in the Cloud more in terms of,

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:01.070
how can we make security
better in the cloud than what

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:01.680
was on-prem?

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:03.910
How do we make it
more automated?

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:05.730
Everybody knows
there is a shortage

00:30:05.730 --> 00:30:07.620
of security professionals.

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:11.525
So how do we get ahead of that
problem by automating it more?

00:30:11.525 --> 00:30:13.650
So it's still early days,
but we're working closely

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:15.358
with our partners so
we can make security

00:30:15.358 --> 00:30:19.090
as seamless as possible,
as automated as possible.

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:21.510
And we're looking forward
to more and more innovation

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:22.259
together.

00:30:22.259 --> 00:30:23.050
SANJAY GUPTA: Sure.

00:30:23.050 --> 00:30:24.930
And maybe, just to
add to that, yes, we

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:29.000
are definitely working
very closely with Google

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:32.220
in that direction, in terms of
building native integrations

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:35.070
into Google services
like Stackdriver,

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:38.670
[? autoscaling ?] function,
building integrations with GKE.

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:40.650
So you can define
your security policies

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:45.270
using Kubernetes label in
GKE, and be able to enforce,

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:46.830
without you having to type--

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.380
learn about IP addresses
or other mechanisms of how

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:50.890
you apply security policies.

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:53.230
So all of that,
essentially, I'd say,

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:55.320
are the foundational
building blocks

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:58.380
which allows you
to have security

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:01.120
move at the speed of the
Cloud, and be very seamlessly

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:03.282
deployed and operated
in these environments.

00:31:03.282 --> 00:31:03.990
VINEET BHAN: Yes.

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:05.150
Thank you.

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:07.420
So, Bill, a couple
of questions for you.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:10.290
I think you had a great
experience deploying

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:13.110
something in GKE.

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:15.200
In the container
world, GKE world,

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:16.680
there are new sets
of challenges,

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:19.200
new kind of securities
people have to think about.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:21.630
And also, companies,
in some cases,

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:24.990
have to rethink how their teams
are organized because things

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:27.780
are moving at a different pace.

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:30.780
So any insights on what
were the new things

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:33.600
that you had to think about, in
terms of security challenges,

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:37.800
in terms of people organization,
how teams should be structured?

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:39.480
It would be especially
helpful for folks

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:41.370
who are new into the GKE world.

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:43.980
And what are the key
things that are very unique

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:45.780
because you are
on the GKE stuff,

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:48.500
or as people are moving into
the more containerized world?

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:50.550
BILL O'BRIEN: Yeah.

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:52.170
I think one of the
biggest challenges

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:55.560
was that it's going
into the cloud, right?

00:31:55.560 --> 00:31:58.260
You just don't know
what is actually

00:31:58.260 --> 00:31:59.760
connected to what, right?

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:02.070
You're just assuming
that, hey, Google

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:03.150
has their stuff together.

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:06.510
And be like, OK, hey,
how can we actually

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:08.606
make sure this is
actually occurring, right?

00:32:08.606 --> 00:32:10.230
And so I was actually
in a presentation

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:11.810
yesterday about compliance.

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:15.710
And one of the things in
there was to trust, but then

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:17.130
to verify, right?

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:21.120
And one of the things of
actually going into GCP

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:23.890
was that it's such an unknown
thing, that it was just like,

00:32:23.890 --> 00:32:24.390
all right.

00:32:24.390 --> 00:32:26.848
At least if we could have one
common thing that we actually

00:32:26.848 --> 00:32:27.582
know--

00:32:27.582 --> 00:32:29.040
so if we were just
like, hey, we're

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:30.415
going to put the
Palo Alto there,

00:32:30.415 --> 00:32:33.144
and we know that everything has
to flow through the Palo Alto,

00:32:33.144 --> 00:32:35.310
then we can actually see
what the flows are actually

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:36.750
going to occur.

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:39.030
So this way, then, we
can actually trust that.

00:32:39.030 --> 00:32:41.490
But then, as a GKE, they
started to spin things up.

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:43.073
And all of a sudden,
it was just like,

00:32:43.073 --> 00:32:45.330
hey, we need connectivity
between here and there,

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:46.011
and so forth.

00:32:46.011 --> 00:32:47.760
So then we actually
started to build that.

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:50.880
So a lot of people who used
to be spending time building

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:54.259
servers and so forth are now
becoming DevOps managers.

00:32:54.259 --> 00:32:56.550
They're working on DevOps to
be able to push things out

00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:57.300
through that.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.360
So even more on the security
side, where it's just like, OK,

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:00.930
hey.

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.270
How can we actually
go to the next level?

00:33:03.270 --> 00:33:06.540
So like Rob had said, about
the native side of it,

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:09.630
where it's just like, OK, hey,
we're going to use the GLBs.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.310
So we already have
[INAUDIBLE] on-prem,

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:12.560
but it's just like, all right.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:14.850
Do we really need another
set of load balancers

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:17.970
out there, where Google already
has load balancers set up

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:19.560
for us to be able to do?

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:23.250
So we basically just
took this step of,

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:25.890
where do we actually
need the tools?

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:27.480
And how do we
actually get to there?

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:30.430
So even going
through the sessions

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:33.132
yesterday with MTLS and so
forth, where it's just like,

00:33:33.132 --> 00:33:35.340
hey, those are going to be
things that we're actually

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:36.750
going to be looking at.

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:38.550
And the biggest
thing within security

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:41.340
is that it's just going to be
layer upon layer upon layer.

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:43.530
And I heard, yesterday,
one of the things

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:45.600
was a really good analogy.

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:49.147
It was a gentleman from Red Hat
who basically had said-- oh,

00:33:49.147 --> 00:33:50.480
I'm sorry, he was from Netflix--

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:52.563
who basically said, you
don't want to build gates,

00:33:52.563 --> 00:33:54.081
but you want to
build guardrails.

00:33:54.081 --> 00:33:56.580
So you want to be able to know
where the traffic is actually

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:57.180
going.

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:59.430
But you don't want to just
put up so many restrictions

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:00.935
that it's just
like, hey, it's very

00:34:00.935 --> 00:34:02.310
cumbersome for
the business to be

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:04.890
able to be flexible, and be
able to get to where it actually

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:07.226
needs to go.

00:34:07.226 --> 00:34:08.100
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:08.812
That goes into--

00:34:08.812 --> 00:34:08.940
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT:
Can I add something?

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:10.244
SANJAY GUPTA: Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:10.244 --> 00:34:10.690
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT:
Yeah, something

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:13.205
that caught my attention
there was trust, but verify.

00:34:13.205 --> 00:34:14.580
In security, you
hear that a lot.

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:19.000
But I think we're moving more
to the zero trust concept.

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:22.116
BeyondCorp was talking about it.

00:34:22.116 --> 00:34:24.449
Palo Alto has been talking
about it for quite some time.

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:26.659
Do not trust anything.

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:30.650
So every packet,
every authentication,

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:32.884
come at that with a
zero trust mindset.

00:34:32.884 --> 00:34:34.550
But you have to do
it in a seamless way,

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:36.110
like you mentioned, Bill.

00:34:36.110 --> 00:34:40.370
So don't put gates up where
gates don't need to be.

00:34:40.370 --> 00:34:43.310
But think about not trusting
everything, instead of

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:45.340
trusting and verifying.

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:47.510
I even get in the habit
of removing that label

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:49.184
called Trust and Untrust.

00:34:49.184 --> 00:34:50.600
Because it gets
you in the mindset

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:52.790
that everything coming
in that interface is

00:34:52.790 --> 00:34:54.627
trusted, when it's not.

00:34:54.627 --> 00:34:56.210
So I just wanted to
make that comment.

00:34:56.210 --> 00:34:59.380
And then also, if you have
to change your architecture

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:03.810
to fit a Palo Alto in or
a Nginx, or another box,

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:05.440
it's probably a bad design.

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:10.510
So you should have a modular
approach, where your GLB--

00:35:10.510 --> 00:35:12.340
your load balancer--

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:13.790
works as it's supposed to work.

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:16.660
And then you could insert your
security tools in between that.

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:19.560
You shouldn't have
to change that.

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:21.930
VINEET BHAN: And
Rob, similar to what

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:25.420
we discussed with Bill, any
kind of organizational changes,

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:27.090
or any structural
changes that you're

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:28.745
seeing because
the Cloud world is

00:35:28.745 --> 00:35:30.120
different from
the On-Prem world?

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:32.590
Or even further in the
container deployments,

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:35.749
are they different from what
VM deployments used to be?

00:35:35.749 --> 00:35:36.540
BILL O'BRIEN: Yeah.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.870
So Cardinal Health actually
has a dedicated Cloud services

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:41.600
team.

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:43.350
And so it's almost
like everybody's title

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:45.210
has Cloud in it now.

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:47.730
So I think it is important
to build that team

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:49.440
and say, hey, we're
going to do this.

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:52.320
We're not just going to act
a bunch of server guys trying

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:54.010
to manage Cloud infrastructure.

00:35:54.010 --> 00:35:58.380
You really have to get
training, education,

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:00.540
and really go all in.

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:02.280
But I think, as you
build that team,

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.560
make sure that
security and finance is

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:06.960
all a part of that ecosystem.

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:09.450
From the DevOps--

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:13.006
I'm big into calling
it DevSecOps, which

00:36:13.006 --> 00:36:14.130
a lot of people don't like.

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:16.588
But I think, eventually, it'll
just be called DevOps again,

00:36:16.588 --> 00:36:19.110
once security has
shifted left, we

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:22.169
have a secure CI/CD pipeline.

00:36:22.169 --> 00:36:24.210
And then, I think it will
be called DevOps again,

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:26.670
at least from a security
perspective, because security

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:28.840
is inherent.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:29.840
VINEET BHAN: Absolutely.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:30.923
I think that's the vision.

00:36:30.923 --> 00:36:34.510
Security being, as Sanjay
was saying, part of the whole

00:36:34.510 --> 00:36:35.640
CI/CD pipeline.

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:37.290
Make it as transparent
as possible,

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:39.420
that people don't even
realize security is there.

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:42.942
I think that's
the nirvana state.

00:36:42.942 --> 00:36:45.900
So Bill, I think there was a lot
of discussion in the keynote,

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:50.970
as well, about Istio, and how
Istio can help with security.

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:54.300
Any thoughts about how Istio
would come into the picture,

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:56.910
and how it can help folks,
and what you guys are

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:57.953
thinking about Istio?

00:36:57.953 --> 00:36:58.760
BILL O'BRIEN: Yeah.

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:02.140
So we went through one of the
sessions yesterday on Istio.

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:06.810
And it's very much in
line with Kubernetes

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:09.480
with how Google has
basically set it up.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:13.200
It just helps you to do
the job of actually helping

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:14.110
the business, right?

00:37:14.110 --> 00:37:15.750
It moves you off
of actually having

00:37:15.750 --> 00:37:23.430
to create in-depth reports,
and it gets you into MTLS.

00:37:23.430 --> 00:37:25.580
And it gets you into
more security focus.

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:28.691
So that way, then, it's just
inherently built into it,

00:37:28.691 --> 00:37:29.190
right?

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:31.260
So as they deploy
the applications,

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.600
as they start to pull
out the development,

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:36.030
it's actually already
integrated into it.

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:39.097
So that way, then, you can set
your policies and controls.

00:37:39.097 --> 00:37:40.680
And it's basically,
you're ready, set.

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:42.810
So we're looking
forward to it, actually.

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.030
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:47.210
So, Sanjay, from a security
company perspective,

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:49.440
where do you see
Istio playing a role?

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.270
And what is Palo Alto
thinking about Istio,

00:37:51.270 --> 00:37:54.810
and how to gel the capabilities
that Istio brings in,

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:58.560
along with the capabilities that
Palo Alto products bring in?

00:37:58.560 --> 00:37:59.440
SANJAY GUPTA: Sure.

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:05.120
So I'd say, I see Istio as
no different than the fact

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:08.540
that, hey, the Google
Cloud platform by itself

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:10.580
provides many of the
native controls built

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:12.740
in from a security point.

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:17.180
Istio is trying to
raise the bar and do

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:23.150
the similar things in the GKE
or Kubernetes environment.

00:38:23.150 --> 00:38:28.580
So we absolutely are looking
to both integrate and co-work

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:31.490
with Istio, which is
Istio is able to provide

00:38:31.490 --> 00:38:34.820
certain value, in terms
of base level security,

00:38:34.820 --> 00:38:37.580
in terms of giving you
visibility on what is happening

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:39.780
in your GKE environment
or, in general,

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:42.600
any Kubernetes environment.

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:46.940
And we're actively working with
the GKE team and the Istio team

00:38:46.940 --> 00:38:51.590
to figure out how
we can insert NG

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:53.630
firewall in these
environments, and what

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:55.480
kind of a value,
over and above Istio

00:38:55.480 --> 00:38:57.770
provides, that we can add.

00:38:57.770 --> 00:39:00.050
And the biggest
area that we believe

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:02.780
is going to be around threat,
and around threat mitigation

00:39:02.780 --> 00:39:05.140
and threat prevention.

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:07.180
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:09.540
I think, in the last
round of questions,

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:11.350
I think I'll start
with you, Rob.

00:39:11.350 --> 00:39:14.710
Any comments on Istio, and
in your concluding remarks,

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:18.325
any key lessons
learned or key points,

00:39:18.325 --> 00:39:20.200
in addition to what has
been discussed today,

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:21.866
that you want to share
with the audience

00:39:21.866 --> 00:39:24.129
so they can benefit
from your experience?

00:39:24.129 --> 00:39:25.170
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT: Yeah.

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:27.160
Istio, I'm really excited about.

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:29.260
The guys here from
Cardinal probably

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:32.380
saw me text them at least
100 times about Istio

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:34.270
in the last few days.

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:36.702
Because I see the
security play, how

00:39:36.702 --> 00:39:38.410
we can do the east
and west segmentation.

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:40.940
We talked a little bit
about north and south,

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:42.955
meaning in from
the internet out--

00:39:42.955 --> 00:39:45.569
or in and outbound
from the internet.

00:39:45.569 --> 00:39:47.860
But there's also a problem
with east and west security,

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:50.710
between your containers,
between workloads.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:55.180
And I think where Istio can
bring that security in between,

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:57.497
is definitely going
to be an advantage.

00:39:57.497 --> 00:39:59.080
Some lessons learned,
I think, go back

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:02.480
to don't change the
architecture based

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:04.250
on a security requirement.

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:07.340
Try to do it transparently.

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:10.045
This is hard for a lot of
security operations teams

00:40:10.045 --> 00:40:11.420
that have their
favorite endpoint

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:14.360
technology, their favorite
tool, that doesn't

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:16.170
work the same way in the Cloud.

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:18.230
I would say, start
to learn other ways

00:40:18.230 --> 00:40:21.140
to do things to get
the same result.

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:24.770
Just don't force legacy-type
tools in the Cloud.

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:26.870
So that was a lesson
learned early on

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:31.430
that we did a little bit of that
I would urge you to not look

00:40:31.430 --> 00:40:32.940
at.

00:40:32.940 --> 00:40:34.610
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:40:34.610 --> 00:40:37.280
And Rob, just a follow-up
question to that.

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:40.254
Did you think, in the
old on-prem world--

00:40:40.254 --> 00:40:41.670
I had met companies
who would say,

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:44.270
we have 120 security
products and more.

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:45.710
Do you think security--

00:40:45.710 --> 00:40:49.250
looking a year out, is
security consolidating,

00:40:49.250 --> 00:40:50.889
or do you see it
still fragmented?

00:40:50.889 --> 00:40:51.930
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT: Yeah.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.220
I absolutely see
it consolidated.

00:40:54.220 --> 00:40:56.430
We're going through
this effort right now.

00:40:56.430 --> 00:41:00.150
We have so many tools, more
tools than I could even name,

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:01.740
that don't really
talk to each other.

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:05.550
They're probably not
utilized, even 20%.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:09.150
So I see a large focus
on consolidation,

00:41:09.150 --> 00:41:11.400
getting the visibility to
the security operations team

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:12.210
that they want.

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:13.930
Don't [INAUDIBLE]
things 100 times

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:15.930
so they have to correlate that.

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:20.080
So simplify that so it takes
minutes to troubleshoot.

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:23.830
But I definitely see a
consolidation of tools.

00:41:23.830 --> 00:41:25.500
VINEET BHAN: Thank you.

00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:26.920
So Bill, similar question.

00:41:26.920 --> 00:41:29.530
Concluding remarks,
any key lessons

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:32.110
learned that you can share
with the folks in the audience,

00:41:32.110 --> 00:41:36.430
and your thoughts of any
consolidation in security.

00:41:36.430 --> 00:41:39.299
Where do you see security headed
in the next couple of years?

00:41:39.299 --> 00:41:40.090
BILL O'BRIEN: Yeah.

00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:42.730
I definitely see security
being consolidated.

00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:46.240
I think everybody is going
to have security basically

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:47.610
somewhere in their title.

00:41:47.610 --> 00:41:52.690
It used to be DevOps security,
network security, even just

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:56.950
physical IT security,
and everything like that.

00:41:56.950 --> 00:42:01.950
I want to look more into the
Google Security command center,

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:04.410
and see how that can actually
help benefit with this.

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:09.080
So even though your title might
not be-- it's just like, hey,

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:10.060
I might be a developer.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:12.220
I might be an
administrator or whatever.

00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:15.010
Security is just such
an important part

00:42:15.010 --> 00:42:16.000
of everybody's job.

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:19.510
And that comes a little bit
from the military side of me,

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:21.175
as well.

00:42:21.175 --> 00:42:23.050
But I think one of the
things for the lessons

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:30.400
learned is the Cloud, Google,
allows you to basically reset

00:42:30.400 --> 00:42:31.460
your environment.

00:42:31.460 --> 00:42:33.700
So that's one of
the biggest benefits

00:42:33.700 --> 00:42:36.205
that I have,
especially with ours,

00:42:36.205 --> 00:42:38.080
where it was like a
monolithic, and we wanted

00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:39.780
to go to micro segmentation.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:42.970
It basically allowed
us to fix known issues

00:42:42.970 --> 00:42:44.030
within the network.

00:42:44.030 --> 00:42:46.332
So instead of actually
spending money and spending

00:42:46.332 --> 00:42:48.040
cap [INAUDIBLE],, or
spending development

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:51.040
time of actually fixing
something on-prem,

00:42:51.040 --> 00:42:53.620
when you go to the Cloud, it
allows you to basically reset

00:42:53.620 --> 00:42:56.504
at that point in time, which is
actually extremely beneficial.

00:42:56.504 --> 00:42:58.420
Because the things that
were basically broken,

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:00.187
you actually have
a chance to fix.

00:43:00.187 --> 00:43:02.020
Things that you actually
want to improve on,

00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:04.430
it gives you a chance to
improve on those items, as well.

00:43:04.430 --> 00:43:06.970
So I think that's one of the
big benefits of actually going

00:43:06.970 --> 00:43:07.900
to the Cloud.

00:43:07.900 --> 00:43:08.775
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:43:08.775 --> 00:43:09.860
Thanks a lot.

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:13.390
So Sanjay, last question
for you, as well.

00:43:13.390 --> 00:43:15.310
You've been in the
security industry,

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:17.630
talking to thousands
of customers.

00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:19.690
Any key insights
on where security

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:22.240
is headed a couple of
years down the road,

00:43:22.240 --> 00:43:24.790
and how Palo Alto is
thinking about security

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:28.380
as it exists in 2020?

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:29.220
SANJAY GUPTA: Sure.

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:30.760
Thanks.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:33.860
So a couple of
observations here.

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:35.380
So one thing I'd
say that clearly--

00:43:38.117 --> 00:43:39.950
a couple of years down
the line, even today,

00:43:39.950 --> 00:43:43.010
it's going to be a
multi-cloud world, right?

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:46.490
Which means you could be
in multiple public clouds

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:51.010
and in your on-prem
environments, as well.

00:43:51.010 --> 00:43:53.470
So I think it is important
for you to think,

00:43:53.470 --> 00:43:57.310
from your security design
architectural perspective, how

00:43:57.310 --> 00:43:59.500
you can play in a
multi-cloud world.

00:43:59.500 --> 00:44:02.920
And I'd say Google is
doing a great job, in terms

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:05.890
of building technologies
like Kubernetes,

00:44:05.890 --> 00:44:08.770
which can allow you
to be highly portable

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:10.900
and operate in this
multi-cloud world.

00:44:10.900 --> 00:44:12.540
And clearly, that
is one area where

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:15.560
we are extremely
focused on ourselves,

00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:18.400
so as to let you
have the flexibility

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:22.420
to operate in this world without
the burden of trying to manage

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:24.280
a fragmented set
of environments,

00:44:24.280 --> 00:44:26.887
and managing hundreds of tools
which are purposely built

00:44:26.887 --> 00:44:27.970
for specific environments.

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:30.550
But it adds tremendous
amount of complexity

00:44:30.550 --> 00:44:33.160
and are not utilized properly.

00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:38.980
So that is one big
area where I see

00:44:38.980 --> 00:44:41.230
that things are going
to move, and which

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:44.900
is going to be relevant
from your point of view.

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:50.260
The second area we see, from
a security perspective itself,

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:53.800
is that security is going to
be more and more of a platform

00:44:53.800 --> 00:44:54.380
player.

00:44:54.380 --> 00:44:57.640
I think that is the point
which both Rob and Bill were

00:44:57.640 --> 00:44:58.840
echoing before.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:02.270
The sense that for the
security to be effective,

00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:05.540
you need to be able to correlate
different independent things

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:09.690
together to be able to drive
the necessary outcomes.

00:45:09.690 --> 00:45:11.925
And best of breed,
point solutions

00:45:11.925 --> 00:45:13.300
do not play in
that world, right?

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:16.696
So it's going to be more
and more of a platform play.

00:45:16.696 --> 00:45:18.070
So you should be
looking at tools

00:45:18.070 --> 00:45:20.500
which can operate in these
heterogeneous environments,

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:22.690
and are able to consolidate
multiple security

00:45:22.690 --> 00:45:24.490
functions together.

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:26.510
And the last point,
though not necessarily

00:45:26.510 --> 00:45:28.930
specifically tied
to what you said--

00:45:28.930 --> 00:45:31.840
but I know that you've
been asking Rob and Bill

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:33.990
about organizations, per se--

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:38.490
is that I think, in
most enterprises which

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:41.320
have been able to
successfully cross the chasm

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:43.510
and move fast into
cloud environments,

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:45.760
there has to be an arc shift.

00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:48.292
Which means it cannot be
teams operating in silos,

00:45:48.292 --> 00:45:50.000
in terms of, this is
the networking team,

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:52.090
and this is my security team,
and this is my application

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:52.670
team.

00:45:52.670 --> 00:45:56.179
The teams are coming
together as a cloud team.

00:45:56.179 --> 00:45:57.970
And in that sense,
also, there is some kind

00:45:57.970 --> 00:46:02.440
of a consolidation, so to speak,
of teams, which is happening.

00:46:02.440 --> 00:46:06.760
And that is very important
to happen for cloud adaption

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:08.075
to be successful.

00:46:08.075 --> 00:46:08.950
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:46:08.950 --> 00:46:11.270
Great, great insights from
everybody on the panel.

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:13.900
So we have the last few minutes
for any kind of questions

00:46:13.900 --> 00:46:16.690
that anybody from
the audience have.

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:18.250
There are mics on both sides.

00:46:18.250 --> 00:46:20.936
Happy to take a few questions.

00:46:20.936 --> 00:46:22.810
And please introduce
yourself and the company

00:46:22.810 --> 00:46:24.145
that you work for.

00:46:24.145 --> 00:46:26.620
AUDIENCE: Hi, my name is Pasir.

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:28.300
The takeaway that
I got from here

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:33.460
is you can't lift and shift the
applications from your on-prem

00:46:33.460 --> 00:46:34.460
into the cloud.

00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:38.310
You have to rebuild it from
almost Cloud Native services

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:39.990
in mind.

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:42.540
With that said,
having tools that

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:44.490
are living in the
data center, would it

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:47.850
make sense to try
to reach their--

00:46:47.850 --> 00:46:50.820
their reach into
the public cloud?

00:46:50.820 --> 00:46:52.650
Or should we just leave
them behind and not

00:46:52.650 --> 00:46:55.600
even try to move those
into the public cloud?

00:46:55.600 --> 00:46:57.850
ROBERT RAVENSCROFT: Louis,
you want to take this one--

00:46:57.850 --> 00:47:00.257
Mr. Lift and Shift?

00:47:00.257 --> 00:47:01.840
AUDIENCE: So at
Cardinal Health, we've

00:47:01.840 --> 00:47:04.630
actually been very successful
in moving our applications,

00:47:04.630 --> 00:47:08.080
as is, from on-prem
into the Cloud.

00:47:08.080 --> 00:47:10.720
Some applications,
depending on how

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:12.980
they're interacting
with the internet,

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:17.430
we need to make a few
slight modifications to,

00:47:17.430 --> 00:47:20.420
like setting up a global load
balancers, things like that.

00:47:20.420 --> 00:47:23.322
But we've migrated hundreds of
applications in non-production.

00:47:23.322 --> 00:47:25.780
We're beginning our phase of
migrating the production apps,

00:47:25.780 --> 00:47:26.577
as well.

00:47:26.577 --> 00:47:28.160
AUDIENCE: And in the
past, these tools

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:29.770
required network
packet visibility.

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:30.850
Is that an issue?

00:47:30.850 --> 00:47:34.630
Is that a thing now, where
you're losing layer one access

00:47:34.630 --> 00:47:35.130
to this?

00:47:35.130 --> 00:47:37.379
Do you still need that,
or is that something

00:47:37.379 --> 00:47:39.045
that you don't even
think about anymore?

00:47:39.045 --> 00:47:42.280
AUDIENCE: We've actually been
able to, using VPC flow logs,

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:45.317
and firewall allow and
deny logs in Google,

00:47:45.317 --> 00:47:47.650
we've been able to provide
our security teams everything

00:47:47.650 --> 00:47:48.581
they've been looking for.

00:47:48.581 --> 00:47:48.822
AUDIENCE: Awesome.

00:47:48.822 --> 00:47:49.786
Thank you.

00:47:54.384 --> 00:47:55.300
VINEET BHAN: Go ahead.

00:47:55.300 --> 00:47:55.841
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:47:55.841 --> 00:47:57.700
This is John Skinner
with Shape Security.

00:47:57.700 --> 00:48:02.590
We provide GCP-hosted AI
to prevent account takeover

00:48:02.590 --> 00:48:04.360
of login servers.

00:48:04.360 --> 00:48:08.170
And my question is
to both Rob and Bill.

00:48:08.170 --> 00:48:11.260
Are there any categories
of data or applications

00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:13.540
that are deemed still
too sensitive to move

00:48:13.540 --> 00:48:14.410
into a public cloud?

00:48:14.410 --> 00:48:16.870
For example, in the
case of Cardinal,

00:48:16.870 --> 00:48:19.150
HIPAA-regulated patient
health records, or perhaps

00:48:19.150 --> 00:48:21.600
the patient login that
sits in front of it.

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:24.910
Or in the case of FTD, as your
whole e-commerce platform,

00:48:24.910 --> 00:48:26.650
and the customer
login sitting in front

00:48:26.650 --> 00:48:28.430
of it, in the public cloud.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:31.221
And if not, why not?

00:48:31.221 --> 00:48:32.720
BILL O'BRIEN: For
us, it's obviously

00:48:32.720 --> 00:48:35.180
PHI and HIPPA, but also GDPR.

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:37.090
And I'd say, absolutely not.

00:48:37.090 --> 00:48:41.920
We can make it much more
secure in the Cloud in minutes,

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:44.260
versus months and years
to do that same type

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:45.860
of micro-segmentation.

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:48.575
So that's my answer.

00:48:48.575 --> 00:48:49.200
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:52.860
For credit card information, and
also for customer information,

00:48:52.860 --> 00:48:55.360
yes, we're going to be
migrating up into the Cloud.

00:48:55.360 --> 00:48:56.854
So it's not there yet.

00:48:56.854 --> 00:48:58.020
But our plan is to do there.

00:48:58.020 --> 00:49:00.629
But we're going to have
additional security around it.

00:49:00.629 --> 00:49:01.170
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:49:01.170 --> 00:49:02.282
Thank you.

00:49:02.282 --> 00:49:03.240
VINEET BHAN: Thank you.

00:49:03.240 --> 00:49:05.175
SPEAKER 1 We have time for
one more quick question,

00:49:05.175 --> 00:49:06.030
and then after that--

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:06.840
VINEET BHAN: So question
on that side, please.

00:49:06.840 --> 00:49:09.840
SPEAKER 1: And then after that,
you can discuss in private.

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:10.920
VINEET BHAN: Thank you.

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:11.250
Sorry.

00:49:11.250 --> 00:49:12.140
Just one last comment.

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:13.470
I think, after the
session, we'll be here,

00:49:13.470 --> 00:49:15.849
so we can take questions
after the session, as well.

00:49:15.849 --> 00:49:16.565
Please go ahead.

00:49:16.565 --> 00:49:18.940
AUDIENCE: I'm Ian, and I'm
the CSO of the Irvine Company.

00:49:18.940 --> 00:49:22.190
And I wanted to follow up a
little bit on that question.

00:49:22.190 --> 00:49:26.940
There's a lot of discussion
in the GDPR world,

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:29.784
and how Google and
Amazon and Facebook

00:49:29.784 --> 00:49:31.950
have the ability, from a
Cloud platform perspective,

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:33.747
to do BI on the back
of the data, right?

00:49:33.747 --> 00:49:35.580
And I know that that's
on the consumer side,

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:37.800
not on the enterprise side.

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:45.450
But nonetheless, you spoke a
little bit about visibility.

00:49:45.450 --> 00:49:49.270
Are there any assurances
that we have that,

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:51.690
below the line of
what we can control,

00:49:51.690 --> 00:50:00.360
that Google has no
access or BI to our data?

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:03.286
Especially the PII stuff--
the health care stuff.

00:50:03.286 --> 00:50:04.410
VINEET BHAN: So absolutely.

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:07.590
And this was a concern that
we have heard from customers,

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:09.310
as well-- other customers.

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:11.910
So there's a lot of work
done on access transparency.

00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:16.890
So things like, every time, if
you guys call customer support

00:50:16.890 --> 00:50:18.990
for Google, and customer
support goes and looks

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:21.400
at anything in
your organization,

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:24.450
any action they take, any page
they visit, will be logged.

00:50:24.450 --> 00:50:26.310
So look at that.

00:50:26.310 --> 00:50:27.245
I think that is--

00:50:27.245 --> 00:50:29.924
a lot of work has been done
to make sure customers feel

00:50:29.924 --> 00:50:31.590
very comfortable
[INAUDIBLE] from nobody

00:50:31.590 --> 00:50:33.450
from Google is looking
into your data.

00:50:33.450 --> 00:50:35.610
Any time they look into
the data because you called

00:50:35.610 --> 00:50:38.200
into support, anything
they did would be logged,

00:50:38.200 --> 00:50:39.450
and you would have visibility.

00:50:39.450 --> 00:50:41.980
And you can see, in almost
real time, what they are doing.

00:50:41.980 --> 00:50:44.740
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:44.740 --> 00:50:45.790
VINEET BHAN: Absolutely.

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:50.030
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:51.500
VINEET BHAN: Absolutely.

00:50:51.500 --> 00:50:52.021
Thank you.

00:50:52.021 --> 00:50:52.520
OK.

00:50:52.520 --> 00:50:53.132
Thanks a lot.

00:50:53.132 --> 00:50:54.590
And thanks a lot
for the panelists.

00:50:54.590 --> 00:50:56.270
I think they had
amazing insights.

00:50:56.270 --> 00:50:58.760
And thanks a lot to Palo
Alto Networks for helping

00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:00.045
to make this session possible.

00:51:00.045 --> 00:51:00.920
Thank you for coming.

00:51:00.920 --> 00:51:01.920
BILL O'BRIEN: Thank you.

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:05.470
